tokenise instead of returning nil

NSLinguisticTagger was 160 lines of stubs whose initWithTagSchemes:options: RETURNED NIL, and iA
Writer calls it 19 times a run. Every one of those got nil back, so every tagger the application
built was dead on arrival and its 15 methods were unreachable behind it.

WHAT THIS IS. A real tokeniser: the string is walked once, characters are classified with the four
NSCharacterSets (letter, decimal digit, whitespace and newline, punctuation), and a run of one kind
is one token. An apostrophe or an underscore between two letters stays INSIDE the word, so dont and
snake_case are one token each rather than three. Sentence ranges come from . ! ? followed by
whitespace. The whole class goes from 15 stubs to 0.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, and the file says so where a caller will read it. There is no part of speech
model here, no lemmatiser and no language identifier. For NSLinguisticTagSchemeTokenType the tags
are EXACT: word, punctuation, whitespace and other are decidable from character classes alone. For
the classification schemes it answers the conservative NSLinguisticTagOther* rather than nil, so a
caller that switches on a tag gets a defined answer it can handle instead of a nil it probably
does not test for.

MEASURED, iA Writer through scripts/app-drive.sh:

  tagger STUB lines  19 -> 0
  faults             0

WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, and this is the honest part. I went here from #194: the library rows say
Updating... forever and the editor pane is empty, and 19 dead taggers a run looked like a candidate.
The rows STILL say Updating... and the editor is STILL empty. The tagger was not what blocked the
library. The fix is real on its own terms, an initialiser that returned nil now returns a working
object, but it did not move the symptom that led me to it.

The character sets are cached in statics rather than fetched per character. They were not on the
first build and that put a message send inside the per character loop.
